[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Strategy Pattern in Python"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Strategy pattern is a behavioral design pattern that lets you define a family of algorithms, put each of them into a separate class, and make their objects interchangeable. The Strategy pattern enables selecting an algorithm at runtime."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Components"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Context:** A class that maintains a reference to a Strategy object. The context is not aware of the concrete strategy being used. It delegates the work to the strategy."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Strategy Interface:** Defines the interface common to all supported algorithms. The context uses this interface to call the algorithm defined by a Concrete Strategy."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Concrete Strategies:** Implement the algorithm using the Strategy interface."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Payment Processing"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's consider a scenario where we need to implement different payment methods (e.g., Credit Card, PayPal, Bitcoin). We can use the Strategy pattern to handle these different payment methods."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class PaymentStrategy(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def pay(self, amount: float):
        pass

class CreditCardPayment(PaymentStrategy):
    def __init__(self, card_number: str, cvv: str, expiry_date: str):
        self.card_number = card_number
        self.cvv = cvv
        self.expiry_date = expiry_date

    def pay(self, amount: float):
        print(f'Paying {amount} using Credit Card: {self.card_number}')

class PayPalPayment(PaymentStrategy):
    def __init__(self, email: str):
        self.email = email

    def pay(self, amount: float):
        print(f'Paying {amount} using PayPal: {self.email}')

class BitcoinPayment(PaymentStrategy):
    def __init__(self, wallet_address: str):
        self.wallet_address = wallet_address

    def pay(self, amount: float):
        print(f'Paying {amount} using Bitcoin: {self.wallet_address}')

class ShoppingCart:
    def __init__(self, payment_strategy: PaymentStrategy):
        self.payment_strategy = payment_strategy
        self.items = []

    def add_item(self, item: str, price: float):
        self.items.append({'item': item, 'price': price})

    def checkout(self):
        total_amount = sum(item['price'] for item in self.items)
        self.payment_strategy.pay(total_amount)

# Usage
credit_card = CreditCardPayment('1234-5678-9012-3456', '123', '12/24')
paypal = PayPalPayment('user@example.com')
bitcoin = BitcoinPayment('1A1zP1eP5QGefi2DMPTfTL5SLmv7DivfNa')

cart1 = ShoppingCart(credit_card)
cart1.add_item('Laptop', 1200.00)
cart1.add_item('Mouse', 25.00)
cart1.checkout()

cart2 = ShoppingCart(paypal)
cart2.add_item('Book', 20.00)
cart2.checkout()

cart3 = ShoppingCart(bitcoin)
cart3.add_item('Keyboard', 75.00)
cart3.checkout()"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Using the Strategy Pattern"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Flexibility:** Allows you to switch between algorithms at runtime."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Open/Closed Principle:** You can add new strategies without modifying the context."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"**Code Reusability:** Strategies can be reused in different contexts."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"When to Use the Strategy Pattern"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When you need to use different variants of an algorithm within an object."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When you have many similar classes that only differ in the way they execute some behavior."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When you want to isolate the business logic of an algorithm from the rest of the code."}]
